### 11\. Cryptographic and temporal rules

The system needs to distinguish four different properties:

| Mechanism         | What it establishes                                   |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- |
| Hash              | Content consistency against a known digest            |
| Digital signature | Authorization or attribution by a signing key         |
| Validator quorum  | Distributed agreement under the consensus assumptions |
| Provenance record | Documented source and custody history                 |

None independently proves the truth of an external claim.

For temporal operations, the architecture should retain both:

* A UTC wall-clock timestamp for chronology.

* A monotonic clock measurement for reliable interval and scheduler operation.

The EC64.44 profile may provide an additional application-level temporal coordinate. It must not replace reliable operating-system timing or be used as the sole basis for cryptographic security.

### 12\. Acceptance criteria

The first implementation should not be considered complete until it passes the following tests.

| ID   | Test                           | Expected outcome                      |
| ---- | ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------- |
| CP01 | Input record ingestion         | Source record preserved               |
| CP02 | Adaptive genre allocation      | Only registered genres assigned       |
| CP03 | Input/output enforcement       | Invalid category rejected             |
| CP04 | Forward processing             | Deterministic result                  |
| CP05 | Reverse processing             | Deterministic result                  |
| CP06 | Four-phase sequencing          | Invalid phase transition rejected     |
| CP07 | Neutral element handling       | Neutral tally without source deletion |
| CP08 | Discrepancy detection          | Difference recorded                   |
| CP09 | Colorization                   | Metadata matches assigned status      |
| CP10 | Binary decision                | Correct policy branch selected        |
| CP11 | BAI resource boundary          | Over-limit operation stopped          |
| CP12 | BAM missing evidence           | Operation deferred                    |
| CP13 | QPDP unauthorized construction | Construction blocked                  |
| CP14 | QPDP authorized construction   | Component manifest committed          |
| CP15 | Provenance validation          | Source lineage retained               |
| CP16 | Signature verification         | Invalid signature rejected            |
| CP17 | Seven-validator quorum         | Five valid signatures required        |
| CP18 | Storage tampering              | Integrity failure detected            |
| CP19 | Annual revision                | Earlier version preserved             |
| CP20 | Recovery                       | Only verified checkpoint restored     |

The seven-validator configuration is a reference quorum model, not by itself a complete PBFT network. The existing simulator will need actual proposal, prepare, commit, and view-change behavior before it can claim full Byzantine consensus.

### 13\. Implementation sequence

I recommend building Cadius Paridium in these increments:

* v0.1 — Data model: genre registry, input/output categories, allocation manifests, and JSON Schema validation.

* v0.2 — Four-phase engine: forward/reverse processing, comparisons, discrepancies, and convergence.

* v0.3 — DBQB: BAI, BAM, equilibrium, neutral tally, and suspension.

* v0.4 — Binary Rumbus: decision tree and policy-controlled branch selection.

* v0.5 — QPDP: component manifests, isolated provisioning, and construction verification.

* v0.6 — Chronology and geometry: Periphery Chronicles, annual versioning, and geometric metadata.

* v0.7 — Distributed integrity: CBBC consensus integration and PPSA persistence.

Completion doctrine:

> Classify adaptively. Allocate bidirectionally. Evaluate through four phases. Maintain equilibrium through bounded autonomy. Construct only through authorization. Preserve every transformation through provenance.

